---
import { codeToHtml } from "shiki";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { colors, shikiTheme } from "../playground/theme.js";

// The formatted pane is squill's real output for the input pane
// (Rust host, default options) — keep them in sync if either changes.
const before = `pub async fn recent_builds(
\tpool: &PgPool,
\tworkspace: Uuid,
) -> sqlx::Result<Vec<Build>> {
\tsqlx::query_as!(
\t\tBuild,
\t\tr#"SELECT wb.id, wb.build_number, w.name FROM workspace_builds wb JOIN workspaces w ON w.id = wb.workspace_id WHERE wb.workspace_id = $1 AND wb.created_at > now() - interval '30 days' ORDER BY wb.build_number DESC LIMIT 25"#,
\t\tworkspace,
\t)
\t.fetch_all(pool)
\t.await
}`;

const after = `pub async fn recent_builds(
\tpool: &PgPool,
\tworkspace: Uuid,
) -> sqlx::Result<Vec<Build>> {
\tsqlx::query_as!(
\t\tBuild,
\t\tr#"
\t\tselect wb.id, wb.build_number, w.name
\t\tfrom workspace_builds wb join workspaces w on w.id = wb.workspace_id
\t\twhere wb.workspace_id = $1 and wb.created_at > now() - interval '30 days'
\t\torder by wb.build_number desc
\t\tlimit 25
\t\t"#,
\t\tworkspace,
\t)
\t.fetch_all(pool)
\t.await
}`;

const rust = (code) => codeToHtml(code, { lang: "rust", theme: shikiTheme });
const sql = (code) => codeToHtml(code, { lang: "sql", theme: shikiTheme });
const inner = (html) =>
	html.replace(/^[\s\S]*?<code[^>]*>/, "").replace(/<\/code>[\s\S]*$/, "");

const beforeHtml = await rust(before);

// In the formatted pane, splice real SQL highlighting into the query
// body (in an editor that's a language plugin's job; here it's done by
// hand at build time). The host lines stay Rust-highlighted.
const lines = after.split("\n");
const open = lines.findIndex((line) => line.endsWith('r#"'));
const close = lines.findIndex((line, i) => i > open && line.includes('"#'));
const head = inner(await rust(lines.slice(0, open + 1).join("\n")));
const body = inner(await sql(lines.slice(open + 1, close).join("\n")));
// A throwaway `r#"` line keeps the tail's `"#,` lexing as a raw-string
// close instead of an unterminated string; dropped after highlighting.
const tail = inner(
	await rust(['\t\tr#"', ...lines.slice(close)].join("\n")),
).replace(/^<span class="line">[\s\S]*?<\/span>\n/, "");
const afterHtml = `<pre class="shiki squill-dark" style="color:${colors.fg}"><code>${head}
${body}
${tail}</code></pre>`;

---

<Base>
	<header>
		<div class="masthead">
			<img src="squill.svg" width="64" height="64" alt="" />
			<div>
				<h1>squill</h1>
				<p class="tagline">
					A robust, friendly, and flexible formatter for SQL code everywhere.
				</p>
			</div>
			<nav>
				<span class="badge">v0.2.2 · MPL-2.0</span>
			</nav>
		</div>
		<p>
			squill (/skwɪl/) is a formatter for SQL code that's built to reliably
			format SQL wherever it may live. It uses Tree-sitter grammars to locate
			your SQL code, even when it's embedded in another language. It plucks
			your queries out surgically and formats them in-place without disturbing
			your other code. It aims to support all features of Postgres and SQLite,
			however you use them.
		</p>
		<p>
			Every statement squill rewrites is{" "}
			<em>self-checked</em>: the output must re-lex to the same tokens —
			allowing only named, verified transforms like keyword case and pg_dump's
			clause order — and keep every comment, or squill emits your original
			bytes untouched. Output is never less correct than input.
		</p>
		<p class="flower">
			named for the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scilla_siberica"
				>Siberian squill</a
			>
		</p>
	</header>

	<div class="pair">
		<figure>
			<figcaption>input <span>rust · sqlx</span></figcaption>
			<div set:html={beforeHtml} />
		</figure>
		<figure>
			<figcaption>
				output <span class="tidy">✓ formatted</span>
			</figcaption>
			<div set:html={afterHtml} />
		</figure>
	</div>

	<section>
		<h2>Get started</h2>
		<pre class="dark"><code>mise use -g github:aslilac/squill

squill fmt --check .</code></pre>
		<p>
			That is the short version; <a href="docs/install/">Install</a> covers Nix,
			Cargo, and release archives, and what to run in CI.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Where to go next</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<a href="docs/install/">Install</a> — mise, Nix, Cargo, release archives.
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href="docs/configuration/">Configuration</a> — every option, where
				settings come from, and per-language sections.
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href="docs/embedded/">Embedded SQL</a> — the host languages squill
				understands, which strings it will rewrite, and how to write your own
				tree-sitter extraction query.
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href="docs/frozen/">Frozen paths</a> — formatting new migrations without
				ever rewriting the ones that already shipped.
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href="playground/">Playground</a> — try it in the browser; your
				input stays on your machine.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>SQL in your source code</h2>
		<p>
			squill also formats SQL embedded in host languages, located with
			tree-sitter queries. Only multiline string syntaxes are rewritten;
			anything with interpolation holes is never touched.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<div style="display: inline-flex; gap: 4px; align-items: center;">
					<img style="height: 16px;" src="langs/rust.svg" />
					<code class="boxed">sqlx::query!</code> and <code class="boxed"
						>sqlx::query_as!</code
					> in Rust
				</div>
			</li>
			<li>
				<div style="display: inline-flex; gap: 4px; align-items: center;">
					<img style="height: 16px;" src="langs/go.svg" />
					<code class="boxed">"database/sql"</code> in Go
				</div>
			</li>
			<li>
				<div style="display: inline-flex; gap: 4px; align-items: center;">
					<img style="height: 16px;" src="langs/typescript.svg" />
					<code class="boxed">sql`&hellip;`</code> templates in JavaScript/TypeScript
				</div>
			</li>
			<li>
				<div style="display: inline-flex; gap: 4px; align-items: center;">
					<img style="height: 16px;" src="langs/gleam.svg" />
					<code class="boxed">sqlight</code>/<code class="boxed">pog</code> in
					Gleam
				</div>
			</li>
			<li>
				<div style="display: inline-flex; gap: 4px; align-items: center;">
					<img style="height: 16px;" src="langs/python.svg" />
					<code class="boxed">cursor.execute</code> in Python (<code
						class="boxed">%s</code
					> placeholders preserved)
				</div>
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			<a href="docs/embedded/">How it works, and how to point it at your own APIs</a
			>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<p>
		It's quick, too: the entire SQL corpus of <a
			href="https://github.com/coder/coder">coder/coder</a
		> — 1,255 files, 36,000+ lines of migrations, queries, and fixtures — formats
		in about <strong>215&nbsp;ms</strong>. A single 15,000-line file takes <strong
			>94&nbsp;ms</strong
		>. Fast enough for pre-commit, save-on-format, everything.
	</p>
</Base>
